ponsors\nVeterinarians (rDVMs)\nSupport the BBVCTO\nReferring Veterinarians\nResources\nAbout\nBack to Main Menu\nAbout\nAbout Overview\nCareers\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nLocations\nGiving\nEmergency\nI am a...\nToggles the 'I am a' Menu with links to the OSU Veterinary Medical Center and the College of Veterinary Medicine\nI am a...\nToggles the 'I am a' Menu with links to the OSU Veterinary Medical Center and the College of Veterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Medical Center\nCompanion Animal Owner\nCompanion Animal Services\nHummel & Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nClinical Trials\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nFarm Animal Services\nHospital for Farm Animal\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\nEquine Services\nGalbreath Equine Center\nReferring Veterinarian\nReferring Veterinarian Portal\nPartnering With You\nClinical Trials\nJob Seeker\nCareers\nAbout the VMC\nContact Us\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nProspective Student\nAcademic Programs\nFinancial Aid\nBuckeye Vet Camp\nMiddle School and High School Prep\nResidency Programs\nCurrent Student\nFinancial Aid\nClass Schedule\nAcademic Programs\nVeterinary Student Research Opportunities\nCollege Research Day\nHealth and Wellbeing Resources\nAlumni or Friend\nAlumni Section\nAlumni Society\nGet Involved\nGiving\nContinuing Education\nResearcher\nSignature Programs\nGrant Support\nEnvironmental Health and Safety\nVeterinary Bioscience Services\nFaculty or Staff Member\nHR.OSU.EDU\nCVM Community\nHealth and Wellbeing Resources\nVIS - Veterinary Information Systems\nWorkday\nCompanion Animal Owner\nBack\nCompanion Animal Owner\nCompanion Animal Services\nHummel & Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nClinical Trials\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nBack\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nFarm Animal Services\nHospital for Farm Animal\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\nEquine Services\nGalbreath Equine Center\nReferring Veterinarian\nBack\nReferring Veterinarian\nReferring Veterinarian Portal\nPartnering With You\nClinical Trials\nJob Seeker\nBack\nJob Seeker\nCareers\nAbout the VMC\nContact Us\nBuckeyeLink\nFind People\nWebmail\nSearch Ohio State\nClose Emergency Menu\nNeed immediate care?\nCompanion\nEmergency\n(614) 889-8070\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nAfter-hours urgent care\nEquine\nEmergency\n(614) 292-6661\nGalbreath Equine Center & Ralph Rogan Equine Performance Evaluation Arena\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nFarm\nEmergency\n(614) 292-6661\nHospital for Farm Animals\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nCompanion\nEmergency\n(614) 292-3551\nHummel Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nOpen 24 hours\nEquine\nFarm\nEmergency\n(937) 642-2936\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nLearn more about all of our emergency, urgent, and critical care services\nBlue Buffalo Veterinary Clinical Trials Office\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nMammary tumors in cats\nMammary tumors in cats\nSurgery is the most common treatment used for mammary cancer in cats. The pathologist determines whether surgery has removed all cancer cells many days after the procedure. We need rapid and accurate testing during surgery to detect residual cancer to decrease cancer recurrence and the necessity for repeated surgery or treatments.\nPolarization-sensitive optical coherence tomography (PS-OCT) is an imaging technology that uses near-infrared light waves to generate real-time, high-resolution images of the microscopic structure of tissues, specifically looking at the organization of the tissues. We have performed some initial evaluations using this optical coherence tomography for detection of residual cancer, which has had very encouraging results.\nCats will undergo surgery to remove their tumor, following removal the tumor will be scanned with polarization sensitive optical coherence tomography to assess the surgical margins for residual cancer cells. The specimen will then undergo thorough histopathological assessment.\nThe overall purpose of the study is to evaluate a new imaging method to detect cancer cells left behind following the removal of mammary tumors in client-owned cats undergoing surgery. This technology has been used successfully in human breast cancer surgery, providing microscopic assessment of surgical margins within minutes. Identifying a rapid and thorough imaging method to detect any remaining cancer cells during surgery for sarcomas especially mammary tumors will support targeted treatment management decisions and improve outcomes for cats and dogs with cancer.\nClient Compensation:\nPet owners are financially responsible for all costs associated with evaluation and surgery. Each pet owner receiving surgery at OSU will receive $70 credit to their account to cover the costs of the histopathology evaluation.\nPotential Medical Benefits:\nThis research will open the door to veterinarians having the technology to allow accurate real-time interpretation of surgical margins to minimize the necessity for additional surgeries or other treatments and decrease tumor recurrence.\nPotential Medical Risks:\nRisks associated with surgery and anesthesia.\nWhat qualifies my pet for enrollment?\nInclusion Criteria:\nEligible patients (any breed of cat) must meet all the following:\nHave cytology or histopathology confirmation of mammary tumor.\nUndergo surgical excision of mammary tumor at OSU.\nExclusion Criteria:\nOther tumors suspected.\nDiagnosis/Condition Being Studied:\nMammary cancer in cats.\nIntervention to Be Studied:\nPolarization-sensitive optical coherence tomography (PS-OCT)\nDownload PDF\nPRE-SCREENING QUESTIONNAIRE\nIf you believe your pet may be eligible to enter this study, please fill out a pre-screening questionnaire.\nPre-Screen Here\nPre-Screen Here\nContacts:\nDr. Laura E. Selmic\nselmic.1@osu.edu\nThe Ohio State University\nVeterinary Medical Center Facebook Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Instagram Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Twitter Page\nVeterinary Medical Center LinkedIn Page\nVeterinary Medical Center YouTube Page\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\n5020 Bradenton Avenue\nDublin 43017 OH\n(614) 889-8070\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\n655 Vernon L. Tharp St\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-1573\nGalbreath Equine Center & Ralph Rogan Equine Performance Evaluation Arena\n685 Vernon L. Tharp Street\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-6661\nHospital for Farm Animals\n601 Vernon L. Tharp St\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-6661\nHummel Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\n601 Vernon L. Tharp Street\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-3551\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\n16410 County Home Rd\nMarysville 43040 OH\n(937) 642-2936\nHelpful Links\nCareers\nContact Us\nPay My Bill\nReferring Vets Portal\nVeterinary Medical Center Facebook Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Instagram Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Twitter Page\nVeterinary Medical Center LinkedIn Page\nVeterinary Medical Center YouTube Page\nLooking for the College of Veterinary Medicine?\nPlease Visit\nvet.osu.edu\nPlease Call\n(614) 292-1171\nNon-discrimination Notice\nPrivacy Statement\nCookie Settings\n©\n2026\nThe Ohio State University\nIf you have a disability and experience difficulty accessing this content, contact\nCVM-DigitalAccessibility@osu.edu",
